    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Low back pain for two days after lifting boxes of books. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Einstein is a 70-year-old man with a 5.0 cm infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm under surveillance, who calls and is seen the same day because of low back pain. Two days ago he carried several boxes of books up a flight of stairs at his office and felt a pulling pain across the right lower back the next morning. The pain is a dull ache, 4 out of 10, worse when he bends, twists, or gets out of a chair, and eased by lying flat and by a heating pad. It does not spread to the abdomen, flank, groin, or legs. He has had no abdominal pain, fainting, lightheadedness, sweating, numbness, weakness, or change in bladder or bowel control, and no fever. He remembered the aneurysm card and called rather than waiting, as he was taught. He has taken acetaminophen 500 mg twice. He takes amlodipine, losartan 100 mg, atorvastatin, and aspirin, and has had no tobacco since his quit date. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>The morning after lifting boxes of books, two days ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Right lower back, without spread to the abdomen, flank, groin, or legs</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Dull ache, worse with bending, twisting, and rising from a chair</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>4 out of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Better lying flat and with heat; acetaminophen helps a little</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No abdominal pain, fainting, sweating, numbness, weakness, bladder or bowel change, or fever</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

